const GLOSSARY=[
['Indemnity','Insurance puts you back in the same financial position as just before the loss — no better, no worse.','A Nairobi shop’s stock worth KES 400,000 is destroyed by fire; under an indemnity policy the insurer pays the value of that stock, not the shop’s lost future profit unless business interruption cover was bought.'],
['Subrogation','After paying you, the insurer takes over your right to recover the loss from whoever caused it.','Your comprehensively insured car is hit by a matatu; your insurer pays for repairs, then pursues the matatu’s third party insurer.'],
['Contribution','When two policies cover the same loss, the insurers share it so you are not paid twice.','A household’s TV is covered under both a domestic package and a separate all-risks policy; the two insurers split the claim.'],
['Proximate Cause','The dominant, effective cause of the loss decides whether it is covered.','Floods during the long rains short-circuit a building’s wiring and cause a fire; whether flood or fire is the proximate cause determines which peril the claim falls under.'],
['Utmost Good Faith','Both sides must honestly disclose everything relevant to the contract.','A proposer who hides a history of two motor accidents risks the insurer avoiding the policy at claim stage.'],
['Insurable Interest','You must stand to lose financially if the insured thing is damaged.','A logbook owner or a bank financing a car has insurable interest; a neighbour does not.'],
['Average Clause','If you under-insure, the insurer pays only the proportion of the loss that the sum insured bears to the true value.','Illustration: stock worth KES 1,000,000 insured for KES 500,000 — a KES 200,000 loss may be settled at KES 100,000.'],
['Excess / Deductible','The first part of each claim that you bear yourself.','A motor policy schedule may state an own-damage excess; you pay that part of the garage bill and the insurer pays the rest. Amounts vary by insurer and are set in the schedule.'],
['Underwriting','The insurer’s process of assessing a risk and deciding terms and price.','An underwriter reviews a warehouse’s construction, fire-fighting equipment and location before quoting industrial fire cover.'],
['Reinsurance','Insurance bought by insurers to share large or many risks with reinsurers.','A Kenyan general insurer writing an airline’s hull cover places most of the risk with reinsurers such as Kenya Re or international markets.'],
['Gross Written Premium','Total premiums an insurer writes in a period before reinsurance.','IRA statistics rank insurers by gross premium; size alone says nothing about claims service.'],
['Net Earned Premium','Premium kept after reinsurance, and relating to the part of the cover period that has passed.','A 12-month policy written in October has earned only about a quarter of its premium by 31 December.'],
['Loss Ratio','Claims incurred divided by earned premium.','Medical classes in Kenya are reported to carry high loss ratios because claims frequency is high.'],
['Combined Ratio','Loss ratio plus expense ratio; above 100% means an underwriting loss.','IRA data reported a general insurance underwriting loss for 2025 — consistent with a combined ratio above 100% for the segment.'],
['Policy Excess','The excess amount stated in a specific policy schedule (see Excess).','Some motor policies carry a separate, higher excess for young or inexperienced drivers — check the schedule.'],
['Material Fact','Any fact that would influence an insurer’s decision to accept a risk or set its price.','Using a private car for ride-hailing is a material fact that changes the risk from private to commercial use.'],
['Claims Reserve','Money set aside for claims reported but not yet paid, and for claims incurred but not yet reported (IBNR).','After a major storm in December, insurers raise reserves for claims they expect to receive in January.'],
['Salvage','The damaged property, or its value, that passes to the insurer after a total-loss settlement.','A written-off car becomes the insurer’s salvage and may be sold at auction.'],
['Betterment','A deduction where a repair leaves you better off than before (e.g. new parts replacing worn ones).','Replacing a five-year-old car’s worn tyres with new ones after an accident may attract a betterment contribution.']
].map(([term,def,ex])=>({term,def,ex}));
